 "drivingQuestion": "Why is he called ben Setada?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Setada is explained as a nickname per Pumbedita usage",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.67a.14",
   "daf": "67a",
   "sentenceId": 13,
   "he": "וְכֵן עָשׂוּ לְבֶן סָטָדָא בְּלוֹד, וּתְלָאוּהוּ בְּעֶרֶב הַפֶּסַח.",
   "en": "«And» the court «did the same to» an inciter named «ben Setada, from» the city of «Lod, and they hanged him on Passover eve.»"
  },
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.67a.15",
   "daf": "67a",
   "sentenceId": 14,
   "he": "בֶּן סָטָדָא? בֶּן פַּנְדִּירָא הוּא! אָמַר רַב חִסְדָּא: בַּעַל סָטָדָא, בּוֹעֵל פַּנְדִּירָא. בַּעַל? פַּפּוּס בֶּן יְהוּדָה הוּא! אֶלָּא, אִמּוֹ סָטָדָא. אִמּוֹ? מִרְיָם מְגַדְּלָא נְשַׁיָּא הֲוַאי! כִּדְאָמְרִי בְּפוּמְבְּדִיתָא: ״סְטָת דָּא מִבַּעְלַהּ״.",
   "en": "The Gemara asks: Why did they call him «ben Setada,» when «he was the son of Pandeira? Rav Ḥisda says:» Perhaps his mother’s «husband,» who acted as his father, was named «Setada,» but his mother’s «paramour,» who fathered this mamzer, was named «Pandeira.» The Gemara challenges: But his mother’s «husband was Pappos ben Yehuda,» not Setada. «Rather,» perhaps «his mother» was named «Setada,» and he was named ben Setada after her. The Gemara challenges: But «his mother was Miriam, who braided women’s hair.» The Gemara explains: That is not a contradiction; Setada was merely a nickname, «as they say in Pumbedita: This one strayed [setat da] from her husband.»"
  }
 ],
